{"protocol":"mindblown-brain/1","brain":"programming","page":{"id":"dsa/recursion","title":"Recursion","type":"concept","lobe":"dsa","desc":"A function defined in terms of itself, with a base case to stop. Natural for trees and divide-and-conquer; limited by stack depth unless the language eliminates tail calls.","intro":"A function defined in terms of itself, with a base case to stop. Natural for trees and divide-and-conquer; limited by stack depth unless the language eliminates tail calls.","created":"2026-09-01","updated":"2026-11-02","synapses":[{"id":"dsa/recursion#1","page":"dsa/recursion","lead":"Base case first","text":"Every recursive definition needs a case that does not recurse; forgetting it is the classic stack overflow.","section":"Synapses","links":["sys/memory/stack-heap"],"cites":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recursion_(computer_science)"]},{"id":"dsa/recursion#2","page":"dsa/recursion","lead":"Tail calls","text":"When the recursive call is the last thing a function does, Scheme and some compilers reuse the frame; CPython deliberately does not.","section":"Synapses","links":["lang/lisp","lang/python"],"cites":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail_call"]}],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recursion_(computer_science)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail_call"],"sections":["Synapses"]},"url":"https://mindblown.pplx.app/programming.html#n=dsa%2Frecursion","parent":null,"children":[],"synapses":[{"id":"dsa/recursion#1","page":"dsa/recursion","lead":"Base case first","text":"Every recursive definition needs a case that does not recurse; forgetting it is the classic stack overflow.","section":"Synapses","links":["sys/memory/stack-heap"],"cites":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recursion_(computer_science)"]},{"id":"dsa/recursion#2","page":"dsa/recursion","lead":"Tail calls","text":"When the recursive call is the last thing a function does, Scheme and some compilers reuse the frame; CPython deliberately does not.","section":"Synapses","links":["la